# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 has "done_involuntarily" and "done_involuntarily → do_without" and "do_without → ◇do_without"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 have "done_involuntarily"; they differ when only "nineteen-twentieths of mankind do without happiness involuntarily" is true, which the second allows
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S2 is read mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 with no modality; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2, to: Suppose "nineteen-twentieths of mankind do without happiness involuntarily" is true and "one does without happiness" and "possibly, one does without happiness" are false. Is S2, "Unquestionably it is possible to do without happiness; it is done involuntarily by nineteen-twentieths of mankind, even in those parts of our present world which are least deep in barbarism; and it often has to be done voluntarily by the hero or the martyr, for the sake of something which he prizes more than his individual happiness.", true then?
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 3 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 has "increases_happiness → admirable" and "¬increases_happiness → ¬admirable"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 has "renounce ∧ increases_happiness → admirable"; they differ when only "the renunciation contributes worthily to increase the amount of happiness in the world" is true, which the second allows; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 has "renounce ∧ increases_happiness → admirable" and "renounce ∧ ¬increases_happiness → ¬admirable"
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered yes, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the renunciation contributes worthily to increase the amount of happiness in the world" is true and "the one who renounces the personal enjoyment of life is deserving of admiration" and "one abnegates for oneself the personal enjoyment of life" are false. Is S6, "All honour to those who can abnegate for themselves the personal enjoyment of life, when by such renunciation they contribute worthily to increase the amount of happiness in the world; but he who does it, or professes to do it, for any other purpose, is no more deserving of admiration than the ascetic mounted on his pillar.", true then?
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 has "◇renounce"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S7 is read mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 with no modality; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 as what is necessary or possible: its "may" or "can" or "should" does not say which it means
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2, to: Suppose "possibly, one abnegates for oneself the personal enjoyment of life" is false. Is S7, "He may be an inspiriting proof of what men can do, but assuredly not an example of what they should.", true then?
mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 has "O renounce → admirable"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 has "done_involuntarily → do_without"; they differ when only "nineteen-twentieths of mankind do without happiness involuntarily" and "the one who renounces the personal enjoyment of life is deserving of admiration" are true, which the first allows; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 has "done_involuntarily → do_without" and "¬admirable → ¬O renounce"
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 3 ways: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 has "¬increases_happiness → ◇renounce ∧ ¬O renounce"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 has "renounce ∧ ¬increases_happiness → ¬admirable"; they differ when only "the one who renounces the personal enjoyment of life is deserving of admiration", "one abnegates for oneself the personal enjoyment of life" and "possibly, one abnegates for oneself the personal enjoyment of life" are true, which the first allows; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 has "renounce ∧ ¬increases_happiness → ◇renounce ∧ ¬O renounce"
mill-utilitarianism-1.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: the conclusion is read mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 with no modality; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 as both: its "may" or "can" or "should" does not say which it means
mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 put it at S7, "He may be an inspiriting proof of what men can do, but assuredly not an example of what they should."; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 at S6, "All honour to those who can abnegate for themselves the personal enjoyment of life, when by such renunciation they contribute worthily to increase the amount of happiness in the world; but he who does it, or professes to do it, for any other purpose, is no more deserving of admiration than the ascetic mounted on his pillar."
mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2, to: Is the text's main conclusion S7, "He may be an inspiriting proof of what men can do, but assuredly not an example of what they should.", rather than S6, "All honour to those who can abnegate for themselves the personal enjoyment of life, when by such renunciation they contribute worthily to increase the amount of happiness in the world; but he who does it, or professes to do it, for any other purpose, is no more deserving of admiration than the ascetic mounted on his pillar."?
mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if it ought to be that one abnegates for oneself the personal enjoyment of life, then the one who renounces the personal enjoyment of life is deserving of admiration"; mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.2 does not
mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2 and mill-utilitarianism-1.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if it ought to be that one abnegates for oneself the personal enjoyment of life, then the one who renounces the personal enjoyment of life is deserving of admiration"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
